## 1. Introduction
Coordination polymers (CPs) and the related metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) have received great scientific attention during the last decade.1 Both have a hybrid nature, being built from inorganic metal cations or cluster-like arrangements linked via the coordination of multitopic organic ligands, granting them a host of properties such as porosity2 or magnetism.3 Possible areas of application include gas storage,4 heterogeneous catalysis5 and sensors.6 The exact definition what constitutes a CP or a MOF has been the source of controversy in the last few years. A recent recommendation of the IUPAC includes that potential voids have to be present for a MOF. All other dimensionally linked coordination compounds can be called coordination polymers.1
A feature of a number of CPs and MOFs that has recently come to greater attention is photoluminescence (PL), the emission of light upon photoexcitation, a phenomenon that has been widely employed in analytical and biomedical applications, studies of excited states of matter, lasers and solid state lighting.7 Combining this property with the versatility of CPs and MOFs is an attractive research goal.
As we will explain in greater detail in the next section, luminescence in CPs can have a number of different, often interplaying sources. In this tutorial review, we would like to outline how metal-based luminescence in CPs and MOFs can be achieved through the correct choice of metals and ligands, what pitfalls can be avoided and how other classes of luminescent materials, solid state phosphors and molecular metal complexes can provide new directions in the synthesis of luminescent CPs. While we will give the reader a number of recent studies on luminescent CPs and MOFs, we would like to refer to reviews by Allendorf,8 Binnemans,9 Chen10 and Rocha11 et al. for a comprehensive view on this topic.
## 2. Concepts of luminescence
Luminescence, the ‘‘spontaneous emission of radiation from an electronically or vibrationally excited species not in thermal equilibrium with its environment’’,12 is a complex phenomenon. Excited species may be generated in a number of different ways, from chemical or electrode reactions, within biological systems, through mechanical forces, by high energy radiation or photons. The photon-induced photoluminescence shall be the focus of our considerations here. A general distinction can be made between semiconductor-based luminescence and the luminescence stemming from metal ions, organic molecules or coordination complexes. The general mechanisms for these two types are outlined in Fig. 1. The ground and excited states evolve from the MOs of organic molecules and coordination complexes towards delocalized states within the conduction and valence of semiconductors. The principal mechanism is identical in all cases, being the absorption of a photon to raise an electron from the ground to an excited state and the subsequent return to the ground state with the emission of a photon, with nanoparticles spanning the gap between molecules and semiconductors. Yet, the detailed mechanisms involved are complex and differ for each case.
In order to keep a presentable scale for this review, we would like to exclude semiconductor-, nanoparticle-, clusterand defect-based luminescence phenomena from our detailed deliberations, as they do not enforce metal participation. Some of these effects have been described for CPs and MOFs, but they are beyond the scope of this review. Yet, the reader is advised to keep these possible sources in mind, especially when observing unusual or unexpected luminescence. An example will be given in Section 4.
In general, a distinction can be made between two main luminescence phenomena: fluorescence and phosphorescence. Whilst fluorescence describes the direct re-emission of absorbed light, phosphorescence covers delayed emission including processes like intersystem crossing. The excited states involved are outlined in Fig. 2 in the classic format of a Jablonski diagram together with the respective transitions. While easily distinguished in theory and quite useful in deciphering overall luminescence mechanisms within a material, a definite differentiation between fluorescence and phosphorescence requires luminescence lifetime measurements. These need to be performed on an experimental setup requiring a defined energy source for selective excitation (e.g. a pulsed laser) and the possibility of time-dependent investigations on the decay time of the luminescence process and intensity. Such investigations are unfortunately often omitted.
