It is one of the peculiarities of CP and MOF luminescence phenomena that a description derived from molecular complexes is often unable to completely account for all effects observed. For example, luminescence can also originate from host–guest interactions or interactions between interpenetrating frameworks. Furthermore, the solid state character of MOFs and CPs also includes the formation of energy bands rather than single discrete energy levels that can be quantummechanically separated. Nonetheless, a ‘‘molecular’’ description is usually a good starting point and in many cases sufficient for a basic explanation of the observed luminescence. Fig. 3 shows the basic phenomena of excitation for molecular complexes, which we will explain in their relation to CP and MOF luminescence.
Ligand-centered (LC) emission is based on the linking organic molecule used in the assembly of a CP or MOF. In principle, it does not need to differ significantly from the luminescence of the free ligand, but often benefits from the immobilization and separation within the crystal lattice.
The metal ions used to construct the CP may exert a heavy atom effect and steer ligand luminescence from the short-lived fluorescence of the singlet state towards phosphorescence from triplet states. While this may be an intended phenomenon, it may also force the system into triplet states where non-radiative deactivation is dominant. Additionally, the immobilization within the CP may enforce a molecular conformation of the ligand in which non-radiative deactivation is increased. When employing carboxylate ligands or other species that may be deprotonated during CP formation, it should be kept in mind that the luminescence of the deprotonated and protonated ligand may differ significantly. To build a CP or MOF with pure LC emission, a fluorescent or a phosphorescent ligand can be chosen and combined with a suitable metal cation or a secondary building unit. Metal cations that usually do not disturb the LC luminescence processes are those with closed shell electron configurations, such as alkaline and alkaline earth ions, transition metal cations with ${\mathsf{d}}^{0}$ or ${\tt d}^{10}$ configurations or group 13 or 14 metal cations such as $\mathrm{In}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ . Additionally, luminescence quenching effects, as outlined in Section 7, should be avoided. CPs with LC luminescence often show interesting effects such as guest dependent luminescence from exciplex or charge-transfer (CT) complex formation.13 Energy transfers between different ligands of a CP can be observed, and are interesting for a comparison to metal-based processes with energy transfers, as shown for the so-called BOB and BOP MOF. Assembled from a pyridyl-functionalized boron dipyrromethene ligand (bodipy) and a tetratopic carboxylic acid together with $\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}$ , replacement of the acid by the porphyrin-based ligand results in a ligand-to-ligand energy transfer shifting the emission from green to red, as outlined in Fig. 4.14 This illustrates the possibility of various energy transfer options in addition to the ones with metal participation, described in the following sections.
Metal-centred (MC) luminescence is predominantly found in CPs and MOFs containing lanthanide or actinide cations, but can also occur in transition and $p$ -block metals, as we will show in Sections 3 and 4.
Luminescence arising from CT states, either as metal–ligand charge transfer (MLCT) for easily oxidized metals and reducible ligands or, vice versa, ligand–metal charge transfer (LMCT) for reversed redox capabilities, is typically found in CPs containing transition metal cations and suitable organic linkers. Other examples exist as well and will be given in the following section.
As stated above, understanding luminescence in CPs can require more complex models than the general one outlined in Fig. 3. Energy transfer between ligands and metals, as shown for the lanthanides in the following section, as well as between different, separate building blocks in heterometallic CPs is often important, as shown in Sections 3 and 5. Luminescence may also arise from more than one excited state, for example combining metal-centred (MC), charge-transfer (CT) and ligand-centred (LC)
emission – sometimes to great benefit for emission colour properties.16 To elucidate the exact mechanisms in a particular luminescent CP, it is often required to perform detailed, temperature-dependent spectroscopic studies on the material and to obtain quantum yields and luminescence lifetimes. Yet, unfortunately, many interesting luminescent CPs are reported only with rudimentary PL and absorption spectra, inferring the mechanism from more or less similar compounds, only, and omitting quantum yields and luminescence lifetimes. The latter two properties are especially important when judging the potential performance in applications. Superficial characterisation of the luminescent properties of CPs and the subsequent ignorance of the mechanisms involved make it difficult to establish general trends in luminescent CPs and identify promising directions towards high performance materials. Thus, we would like to urge the reader to perform detailed characterisations of the luminescent properties of their luminescent CPs and MOFs, whenever applicable.
The design of luminescent CPs can profit from lessons learned in two related fields: solid state phosphors and luminescent metal complexes. Both are broadly investigated and mature fields that have greatly furthered our knowledge of luminescent materials. CPs and MOFs combine characteristics from both areas, as outlined in Fig. 5.
Hence, we will use examples from these fields to illustrate potential new pathways for the synthesis of luminescent CPs and MOFs in the following sections.
## 3. Lanthanide and actinide luminescence
Lanthanide-based luminescence has proven to be a valuable tool in many areas including solid state lighting and biomedicine.17 In general, an overview of lanthanide and actinide luminescence has to distinguish between transition that includes f-states only and the participation of other states like d-orbitals.9
The shielded nature of the f-electrons provides MC f–f-transitions with very little influence from the chemical surroundings of the lanthanide ion. This results in easily predictable and very narrow emissions, as excited and ground states do hardly differ except in energy, covering a range from UV to NIR that is characteristic of $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions, as shown in Fig. 6.
Despite these useful features, obtaining the necessary excited states for lanthanide luminescence in a material can be difficult. Due to their parity-forbidden nature, the absorption coefficients for f–f-transitions are very low, which mostly gives ineffective luminescence.
Different lanthanide elements in their trivalent state show diverse luminescence colours and characteristics. A large number of luminescent lanthanide compounds are based on Tb(III) and ${\mathrm{Eu}}({\mathrm{m}})$ which provide green or red 4f–4f-emissions, respectively. Additionally, $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{m})$ and ${\mathrm{Eu}}({\mathrm{m}})$ ions feature an energy gap between their main emissive and receiving states that is large enough to avoid pronounced vibrational quenching. Lanthanide ions such as $\operatorname{Nd}(\operatorname{m})$ , $\mathrm{Er}\big(\mathrm{III}\big)$ or $\mathrm{Yb(m)}$ can provide f–f-emissions in technologically relevant ranges such as $\mathrm{NIR}^{18}$ or show upconversion effects where two or more NIR photons are combined into one shorter wavelength.
Yet, lanthanides show much richer photophysics than this, as they are not generally restricted to MC f–f-luminescence.19 As an exception, $\mathrm{Ce}\mathrm{(\mathrm{III})}$ can show 5d–4f-transitions as emission in the visible range, as the energetic level of the d states is reduced by ligand and crystal fields. These transitions show a much higher dependence on the coordination environment of the metal ion than f–f transitions, due to participation of d states. They provide broad absorption and emission bands that do not require an antenna effect to improve brightness, as the transitions f–d and $\mathbf{d-f}$ are parity allowed. This is also observed for divalent lanthanide ions like $\mathrm{Eu(n)}$ , as they can also commit 5d levels in luminescence processes. Reduction of energy of 5d states depending on chemical surrounding and the crystal field dominates broad band processes that are parity allowed and therefore as strong as for Ce(III). Hence, transitions involving d and f states significantly differ from f–f-transitions.
To circumvent the low light absorption of f–f-transitions, an ‘‘antenna effect’’ as outlined in Fig. 7 is used frequently. Here, excitation occurs at a suitable ligand, which subsequently relaxes to the triplet state and then transfers energy to the lanthanide ion. In some cases, a direct energy transfer from a singlet state has also been observed. With this mechanism, the overall light absorption can be greatly enhanced, since the parity forbidden f–f transitions during excitation are circumvented. This typically results in much brighter emission, even if the emission still relies on parity forbidden f–f transitions, as the emissive f-states are continuously populated by the energy transfer. The exact photophysics involved here are quite complicated, but have been analysed in great detail for molecular complexes.20 It should be pointed out that brightness and quantum yield of a phosphor should not be confused: whilst brightness concerns both, the number of absorbed photons together with the number of emitted photons, quantum yield is defined as the number of emitted photons divided by the number of absorbed photons. A lanthanide ion may be very inefficient in absorbing photons by f–f-transitions, but it may have a large quantum yield, if this low number of photons is again emitted. As a consequence, the quantum yield of a lanthanide complex showing an antenna effect may be smaller than for a bare lanthanide ion, but it will most likely emit a much brighter luminescence as the material can absorb more photons by allowed transitions.
The ‘‘design’’ of suitable antenna ligands is a vital parameter. A number of principles have emerged from the study of luminescent lanthanide complexes that can help to guide the search for an appropriate ligand antenna. To achieve optimal luminescence quantum yields, a ligand with a $\mathbf{T}_{1}$ state of appropriate energy equalling or slightly lower than the excited Ln-state should be chosen so that the energy difference between the ligand’s triplet state and the excited lanthanide states is around $2500{-}3500\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ . A larger gap makes the energy transfer less efficient, while a smaller gap allows a partial backtransfer of excitation energy from the metal to the ligand with subsequent radiative or non-radiative decay from the excited ligand state. Excited ligand states at lower energy than excited f-states of the lanthanides do not allow energy transfer from the ligand to metal. The energy levels involved can be estimated from a Dieke diagram for the lanthanide(III) ions,21 but have to be determined either experimentally or through time-dependent quantum chemical calculations for the ligand of choice.22
It should be noted that the exact energy transfer mechanism involved in the antenna effect is often of significant interest, although not easily elucidated. At short ranges of $10\mathring{\mathrm{~A~}}$ or less, when an antenna molecule is within the bonding distance to the lanthanide ion, a direct electron exchange via a Dexter mechanism is possible. At longer ranges of up to $10\ \mathrm{nm}$ , for example when an antenna molecule or a complex is embedded as a guest within the framework of a lanthanide-based MOF, a dipole–dipole exchange via a Fo¨rster transfer may be observed.
The construction of CPs and MOFs containing lanthanide ions is different in some aspects from those containing transition metals. In general, lanthanide ions are less predictable by means of a defined coordination sphere thus giving more variable building blocks than their transition metal counterparts, with coordination numbers ranging from 6 to 12. In common room temperature or solvothermal synthetic approaches towards CP formation the co-coordination of water or other oxygen-containing solvents often cannot be avoided. Nonetheless, lanthanide CPs mostly possess interesting and unusual topologies, often forming isostructural series throughout many 4f elements. Luminescent lanthanide CPs and MOFs represent a well-established area, which has already been developed towards applications.23
Actinides containing luminescent CPs and MOFs have been hardly described but, in principle, should follow the same rules as their lanthanide homologues, together with a tendency towards higher participation of other orbitals.
In the following paragraphs, we want to introduce the reader to a number of recent and illustrative luminescent lanthanide and actinide CPs and MOFs. An example of the classic approach towards luminescent CPs has been reported recently: combining $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ salts with 1,10-phenanthroline (phen) and benzimidazole5,6-dicarboxylic acid $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{bidc}\right)$ in water under solvothermal conditions, isostructural 1D CPs $\{\mathrm{[Ln_{3}(b i d c)_{4}(p h e n)_{2}(N O_{3})]{\cdot}2H_{2}O\}_{n}}$ $\mathbf{\dot{L}n=G d}$ , Eu, Tb) are obtained.24 It is interesting to note that only the carboxylate group of the ligand $\mathrm{bidc}^{2-}$ participates in the coordination of the Ln ions, while the nitrogen functionality does not. This shows the oxophilic tendency of lanthanide ions, which has to be taken into account when designing CPs and choosing appropriate reaction conditions and ligands. The coordination of phenanthroline shows that the use of chelating nitrogen donor ligands is one way to circumvent the oxophilicity. The compounds show blue, LC emission for $\mathbf{Ln}=\mathbf{Gd}$ , red MC emission for $\mathbf{L}\mathbf{n}=\mathbf{E}\mathbf{u}$ and green MC emission for $\mathbf{L}\mathbf{n}=\mathbf{T}\mathbf{b}$ . The Gd compound thus allows detailed analysis of the photophysics of the ligands within the CP network. The lack of ligand emissions in the Eu and Tb compound indicates an efficient antenna effect. The quantum yields for the Eu and Tb compounds are $13.20\%$ and $2.08\%$ , respectively, indicating extensive quenching, likely through ${\bf O-H}$ and $\mathbf{N}{\mathrm{-}}\mathbf{H}$ vibrations, as we will discuss in detail in Section 7. Co-doping experiments with all three lanthanides within one compound allow for facile emission colour tuning. In Section 5 we will take a closer look at this technique.
An intriguing example of an ${\mathrm{Eu}}({\mathrm{u}})$ metal–organic framework is $\mathrm{\big[Eu_{2}L_{3}\big(H_{2}O\big)_{4}\big]{\cdot}3D M F}$ $\mathrm{~\bf~\underline{~}{~L~}~}=\mathrm{~\bf~2^{\prime},5^{\prime}~}$ -bis(methoxy-methyl)- $[1,1^{\prime}{:}4^{\prime},1^{\prime\prime}$ -terphenyl]- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime\prime}}$ -dicarboxylate), shown in Fig. 8.25 This compound, featuring a porous, three-dimensional framework structure, can be used as a DMF sensor after exchange of the DMF guest molecules for $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ . Upon exposure to DMF vapor, the exchanged material shows a dramatic increase in Eu(III) emission intensity. This response was found to be selective towards DMF, with exposure to a range of common solvents producing a much smaller increase in luminescence intensity in the material. Through detailed luminescence studies with $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ and ${\bf D}_{2}{\bf O}$ containing materials, it was shown that the increase in luminescence upon DMF exposure is only partly due to removal of quenching $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ molecules. The main factor in the selective luminescence increase is the interaction between the DMF molecules and the ligand L, leading to an improved antenna effect. A prototype sensor fabricated from this compound exhibited response times in the range of a few minutes and good stability over several cycles.
A recent example of NIR-emission from $\operatorname{Nd}(\operatorname{III})$ and ${\displaystyle\Upsilon{\bf b}(\mathbf{\mathrm{\mathbf{\rho}_{\mathrm{III}}}})}$ based CPs is the isomorphous series $\mathrm{[Ln(Hhqc)(ox)(H_{2}O)_{2}]}_{n}$ (with $\mathrm{Ln}~=~\mathrm{Eu}$ , Tb, Sm, Tm, Dy, Nd, Yb, and Gd; ${\bf H h q c}^{-}={\bf\Phi}$ 4-hydroxyquinoline-2-carboxylate, $\mathbf{ox}^{2-}\mathbf{\Omega}=\mathbf{\Omega}$ oxalate). A detailed analysis of the luminescence properties of all compounds shows a weak NIR emission for the Nd and Yb members of the series.26
As an exception, energy can also increase by absorption of two photons. CPs exhibiting such upconversion have remained scarce. A recent example is provided by the solid solution series $[(\mathrm{Tb}_{1-x}\mathrm{Yb}_{x})(\mathrm{DPA})(\mathrm{HDPA})]$ $\mathbf{\dot{H}}_{2}\mathbf{DPA}\ =$ pyridine 2,6-dicarboxylic acid; $\pmb{x}~=~0.05\substack{-0.50}$ . These compounds exhibit a green Tb emission upon excitation of Yb in the IR region at $980~\mathrm{nm}$ .27
Actinide luminescence in CPs and MOFs has not been explored in great detail, likely due to the difficulties associated with handling radioactive materials. Nevertheless, a number of examples of $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}$ luminescence are known. Here, uranium possesses the ${5}\mathrm{f}^{0}$ electron configuration. Thus the observed transitions are assigned to a LMCT state. A recent study highlights a number of compounds based on $\mathrm{UO_{2}}^{2+}$ , terpyridine and different co-ligands. Depending on the energy level of the coligands, luminescence quenching via a back transfer mechanism can be observed in this series.30
As indicated in the chapter before, new approaches of research in lanthanide-containing CPs and MOFs can be identified from the neighbouring fields of luminescent molecular complexes and solid state phosphors. A challenge in luminescent lanthanide CPs and MOFs lies in increasing quantum yields and overall brightness of emission and in tuning of absorption ranges. The macrocyclic ligands employed in brightly luminescent lanthanide complexes may be a new way to approach this problem. In the same way, efficient NIR emitters may be constructed by following the lessons learned from molecular complexes regarding the choice of efficient sensitizers.18 Other luminescence phenomena such as quantum-cutting – the emission of two photons of the half the energy of the absorbed photon – have remained a domain of solid state phosphors, but in principle, should be possible for luminescent lanthanide CPs as well.31
## 4. Transition and main group metal luminescence
In contrast to the narrow and well predictable emissions of f–f-transitions, luminescence in transition and main group metals commonly shows broad bands, sometimes covering several hundred nanometres. Regardless of whether a transition is of MC or CT nature, the excited states in transition and main group metal compounds are distinctly different from the ground state and often considerably anti-bonding, thus producing broad emissions as well as large stokes shifts, similar to those in d–f-transitions in lanthanides. Also, several kinds of luminescence may occur in the same compound, either in distinct form or even as a mixture of states. This often makes identification of the exact nature of the transitions in a luminescent transition or main group metal compound very challenging.32
MC luminescence is often observed for first row transition metals (d–d-transitions) and main group metals (s–p-transitions). Well-researched examples of transition metal compounds showing MC luminescence are chromium(III) complexes.33 Yet, this class of compounds also illustrates the problems commonly associated with MC luminescence stemming from d–d-transitions: the luminescence of these complexes is often quite weak and only observable in rigid matrices at low temperatures and their excited states are often photo-reactive.
LMCT luminescence may occur in transition and main group metal compounds with metals in high oxidation states that can be partially reduced in combination with ligands that can be partially oxidised. Metal ions with closed d-shells like ${\bf d}^{0}$ and ${\mathsf{d}}^{10}$ provide likely candidates where MC luminescence is not dominant. Thus, luminescence in zinc(II)- and cadmium(II)- based CPs and MOFs is often attributed to LMCT. Yet, the unambiguous assignment of LMCT luminescence in CPs is often difficult, especially in mixed ligand systems.
MLCT luminescence is prominent in easily oxidised second and third row TM complexes with $\mathsf{d}^{6}$ , ${\mathsf{d}}^{8}$ and ${\tt d}^{10}$ electron configurations and $\pi$ -acceptor ligands. Numerous detailed photophysical studies of the prototypical MLCT complex $[\mathrm{Ru}(2,2^{\prime}{\cdot}\mathrm{bipyridine})_{3}]^{2+}$ and its derivatives are available.34 Experiences from the chemistry of these molecular complexes have already been successfully transferred to CPs and MOFs. But since these compounds usually take the form of heterometallic CPs and MOFs, we will cover them in the next section.
For the reasons stated above, a transition or main group metal CP or MOF with unambiguous assignment of MC luminescence has remained elusive. The most likely candidates for a clear display of MC luminescence are compounds containing $\mathbf{s}^{2}$ -ions such as lead(II) or bismuth(III). Judging from results obtained for molecular complexes of these ions, it should be possible to observe s–p-transitions in the corresponding CPs.35
Yet, an example outlining the difficulties with clear assignment even in these cases is the kagome´-type 2D coordination polymer Bi(pydc)2 $\mathbf{\sigma}(\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{O})(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O})_{0.83}$ $\mathrm{(pydc^{2-}=}$ pyridine-2,5-dicarboxylate), shown in Fig. 10, displaying a broad blue emission maximum at $481\mathrm{nm}$ . This is a marked blue shift in comparison to the free ligand $\mathbf{H}_{2}$ pydc, which shows a green emission with a maximum at $518~\mathrm{nm}$ . It is suggested that the emission in the CP is due to LMCT and/or s–p-transitions due to conclusions drawn from comparison with literature-known bismuth compounds and through differences between the excitation spectra of the CP and the free ligand. However, even in this comparably simple system containing only one type of ligand, a definite assignment remains elusive.36
A CP displaying an emission assigned as LMCT in nature is the compound $\mathrm{\big[H_{3}O][I n(b t e c)]{\cdot0.8H_{2}O}}$ 1 $\mathrm{\bf{H}}_{4}\mathrm{\bf{btec}}=1,2,4,5$ -benzenetetracarboxylic acid). It shows an emission at $467~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , while the free ligand emits at $340~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .37
The archetypical MOF-5 $\mathrm{Zn}_{4}\mathrm{O}(\mathrm{BDC})_{3}$ ( $\mathbf{BDC}^{2-}=1,4$ -benzenedicarboxylate) represents an interesting case study of the problems associated with the assignment of luminescence mechanisms in MOF chemistry. Early works suggested quantum dot-like luminescence behaviour of the secondary building units, inferred via the comparison with the luminescence of bulk and nanosized ZnO.38 Additional studies later showed that $z\mathrm{no}$ impurities in the material gave rise to the emission assigned to quantum dot-like luminescence and that pure MOF-5 displays a luminescence behaviour that is more closely related to that of the ligand.39 Yet, the exact nature of the luminescence of MOF-5 is still under dispute with LLCT, $^{40}\mathrm{LC}^{39}$ and $\mathrm{LMCT^{41}}$ mechanisms as primary suggestions.
## 5. Heterometallic assemblies and doping
In general, a distinction should be made between heterometallic and doped CPs and MOFs by means of statistic or non-statistic replacement, although this is not always strictly followed in the literature: heterometallic CPs and MOFs contain two or more different metals, each possessing their unique positions within the structure. In doped CPs and MOFs a host lattice is doped with often small amounts of a different metal that statistically replaces the same positions as the metal that constitutes the host material. Fig. 11 shows a schematic drawing of these two cases.
A recent study gives an impressive example of the advances in ${\mathrm{Ru}}({\mathrm{\Omega}}_{\mathrm{{II}}})$ metalloligand based MOFs: employing a carboxyfunctionalized $\mathrm{{Ru}(\Omega_{\mathrm{{II}}})}$ complex L together with $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -biphenyldicarboxylic acid $\left({\mathrm{H}}_{2}{\mathrm{bpdc}}\right)$ in a reaction with zinc nitrate in
DMF gives the phosphorescent MOF $\mathrm{[Zn_{2}(b p d c)_{2}L_{0.5}]{\cdot}9D M F}$  $9\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ . This MOF shows an emission behaviour typical for a $^3\mathbf{MLCT}$ transition in $\left[\mathrm{Ru(bipy)}_{3}\right]^{2+}$ complexes, with an emission maximum at $624~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Measurement of the time-dependent luminescence quenching induced by the diffusion of different-sized amines into the channels of the MOF provides facile insight into the diffusion dynamics of the material, outlined in Fig. 12.43
A number of examples exist for heterobimetallic CPs containing a transition metal complex as a sensitizer for the IR luminescence of lanthanide ions. One of these compounds is $\mathrm{\small{[Ru(bpym)(CN)_{4}][N d(N O_{3})(H_{2}O)_{5}]}}$ $(\mathrm{bpym}=2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyrimidine), a one-dimensional CP forming helical chains. Here, the metalloligand $\mathrm{[Ru(bpym)(CN)_{4}]^{2-}}$ connects the $\mathbf{N}\mathbf{d}^{3+}$ ions via two of the CN groups. Photophysical studies of this compound show a very efficient energy transfer from Ru to Nd resulting in the typical NIR emission of $\mathrm{Nd}^{3+}$ at 1060 and $1340~\mathrm{{nm}}$ when excitation is provided via the $^1\mathrm{MLCT}$ transitions of the Ru complex at 342 and $437~\mathrm{{nm}}$ . Yet, using samples containing ${\bf D}_{2}{\bf O}$ instead of $\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}$ to avoid ${\bf O-H}$ vibrational quenching, it can also be shown that the CN groups likely provide a phonon-assisted pathway for nonradiative deactivation. Evidence is given by a very short luminescence decay time for the Nd emission of $64~\mathrm{ns}$ .44
A series of framework compounds containing $\mathbf{Na}^{+}$ , $\mathbf{Bi}^{3+}$ and benzenedicarboxylates doped with ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ or $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ during synthesis represents rare examples of doped materials in which the constituting metal is not a lanthanide. The resulting materials show characteristic red or green lanthanide luminescence which can be excited via the carboxylate ligand at $305~\mathrm{{nm}}$ .45
Color tuning can also be achieved by enlarging co-doping over several different metals used at once for statistic replacement: the two-dimensional, porous MOF $[\mathbf{G}\mathbf{d}_{2}\mathbf{C}\mathbf{l}_{6}(\mathbf{bipy})_{3}]$ (bipy $\mathbf{\Sigma}=\mathbf{\Sigma}$ $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -bipyridine) serves as a host lattice into which different amounts of ${\mathrm{Eu}}^{3+}$ and $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ have been doped. This allows for facile tuning of the overall emission color via the variation of Eu and Tb, as shown in Fig. 13.46
In a recent example, it was shown that europium co-doping in a white emitting indium MOF can be used to significantly improve the color temperature of the material, an important requirement for an application in solid state lighting.16
Heterometallic assemblies offer great potential to transfer insights from luminescent molecular complexes into the field of coordination polymers and MOFs, as the examples above have shown. Doping, a method that has long been restricted to solid state phosphors, also offers great opportunities for new luminescent CPs and MOFs. Care has to be exercised when directly translating successful host–dopant pairs from solid state phosphors to CPs, since semiconductor and defect-induced luminescence is common in these materials. But suitable combinations beyond lanthanide pairs are known and may be incorporated into CPs and MOFs. This also offers the additional benefit of reduced concentration quenching, allowing for higher amounts of dopant and solid solution series that are effectively luminescent even at high concentration of the luminescent metal.
## 6. MOFs featuring luminescent metal guests
MOFs, particularly those featuring high porosities, suffer from a distinct drawback with regard to efficient luminescence: the ‘‘empty space’’ within the structure cannot contribute to the overall luminescence. Yet, this does not need to be a severe problem, as the example outlined below shows, and may indeed be turned into an advantage: the pores of the network may be filled with luminescent guest molecules and ions. Thus, luminescent metal complexes may be grafted into the MOF in a post-synthetic step or in situ upon framework formation. Several types of interaction are possible between host structures and guest molecules, ranging from formation of coordinative bonds and ionic interactions to weaker hydrogen bonds or van-der-Waals interactions.
A simple and elegant example of this technique has been presented recently: a neutral, zinc-based multi-nodal MOF featuring pores in the micro- and mesoporous range was loaded with $\mathrm{Ln}^{3+}$ ions by soaking samples of the MOF in DMF solutions of the lanthanide ions. The resulting samples show the characteristic emissions of the respective lanthanides. Loading a mix of different lanthanides onto the samples allows for facile tuning of the emission colour of the resulting materials, as outlined in Fig. 15.48
This technique, while producing materials that are susceptible to leaching when used in or with liquids and that might show reduced porosity after loading, offers an alternative to the more elaborate method of doping and co-doping of the metal ions constituting the CP or MOF. Such MOFs especially interesting for applications like sensing as they offer direct contact between luminescent intercalations and adsorbents.
## 7. Quenching phenomena
Quenching phenomena can be an obstacle when designing and synthesizing luminescent CPs and MOFs, rendering materials that should – in theory – possess superior luminescent properties useless or insufficient.
Several phenomena can be distinguished when considering quenching mechanisms.
Vibrational quenching, either direct or via phonons or lattice vibrations, is a commonly encountered mechanism. High energy vibrations of ${\bf O-H}$ and N–H bonds, especially when close to a luminescent metal centre, can severely diminish luminescence lifetimes and quantum yields.9 If they stem from solvents coordinated to the metal centres or located within the voids of a MOF, the situation may be easily remedied by removing or replacing the solvent in question, either during synthesis or in a post-synthetic step. If they have their origin in the ligands used to construct the CP, removing these vibrations may be more difficult, but achievable – at least on a laboratory scale – by employing fluorinated or deuterated reagents. Vibrational quenching through phonons can be much more difficult to assess and remove.
Concentration quenching is a phenomenon which has been well-researched in solid state phosphors. Excitation energy is transferred between luminescence centres and continuing along neighbouring centres until a defect is reached or nonradiative processes set in. Thus, the excitation energy is dissipated without an emission. A low concentration of luminescence centres in a material can suppress this effect. Concentration quenching is usually a lesser issue in CPs and MOFs, since the extended p-electron systems found in many ligands have a shielding effect regarding the cross-relaxation described above. Nonetheless, concentration of luminescence centers also has an influence on MOFs and CPs. Luminescence properties may benefit from reduced concentrations of luminescent metal centres, either through co-doping methods or via separation with extended ligands.
In addition, typical MOF and CP metal ions with open shells such as ${\mathrm{Cu}}^{2+}$ , ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ and $\mathrm{Ni}^{2+}$ as well as radicals such as oxygen can act as strong luminescence quenchers. This has to be kept in mind when designing luminescent CPs and MOFs, as these effects are not beneficial and can completely suppress luminescence. However, recent examples of $\mathrm{\small{[Ln_{2}N i(H b i d c)_{2}(S O_{4})_{2}.}}$ $\left(\mathbf{H}_{2}\mathbf{O}\right)_{8}]_{n}$ $[\mathrm{Ln}=\mathrm{Pr}$ , Sm, Eu, Gd, Tb, Dy, Ho, Er, Yb, $\mathbf{H}_{3}\mathbf{bidc}=$ $1H\cdot$ -benzimidazole-5,6-dicarboxylic acid) exemplify that luminescence is not necessarily quenched completely by open shell ions.49 Therefore, such phenomena can also be used to construct sensors, as has been shown for a $[\mathrm{Ru}(2,2^{\prime}\mathrm{-bipyridine})_{3}]^{2+}$ derived MOF which can act as an oxygen sensor.50
## 8. Applications and future directions
Although luminescent CPs and MOFs have yet to see a larger variety of commercial applications,23 they are of great interest to the future development of fields such as solid state lighting, OLEDs, sensing and sensors.
Solid state lighting has been the domain of inorganic phosphors for the last few decades. Luminescent CPs and MOFs may enhance this field due to several benefits: as an organic–inorganic hybrid material, they are more compatible with organic matrices of OLED applications than inorganic metal oxide and nitride powders. The organic ligands of CPs and MOFs provide sensitizing antennas for metal-based luminescence that are less variable and available in inorganic phosphors and can add an additional LC luminescence to improve overall luminescence properties.
Sensing and sensor applications, a few of which have been presented in the course of this review, can greatly profit from the multifunctional nature of CPs and MOFs. Luminescence, in contrast to a number of other properties that may be used to construct sensors, is a highly sensitive method to observe electronic changes caused by an analyte. Porous luminescent MOFs may then provide the selectivity needed for sensor applications by molecular sieving. And while luminescence quenching is commonly used in prototypical MOF sensing applications, some materials even provide a change in luminescence colour as an observable sensor output – making for an easy readout even with simple equipment and thus presenting good approaches towards real-world applications.
Further development in luminescent CPs and MOFs can be expected from the fields of co-doping of metal ions, reducing quenching by concentration, allowing tuning of the luminescence and creating luminescence in otherwise non- or differently luminescent coordination polymers, as outlined in Section 5. Especially for MOFs, this also includes microporosity and thus the incorporation of luminescent species into framework structures. Thereby, the variety of tuning abilities will largely increase offering further options for sensing of chemical species by changes in the luminescence properties upon sorption processes.
## 9. Conclusions
The large variability of metal–ligand combinations in MOFs and CPs enables multiple possible luminescence processes and has already led to a large number of luminescent materials.
This tutorial review introduces the main concepts of the luminescence processes involved, including ligand and metalbased processes. Focusing on the participation of metals, the review illustrates the differences between core close f-electronbased lanthanide transitions and transitions involving states of metal to ligand bonding, found for transition metals, main group metals as well as a few lanthanides. This leads to dependencies of metal-based luminescence if lanthanides, actinides, other transition metals or main group metals are involved. The metal ligand interactions used to construct CPs and MOFs also allow energy transfer processes between the organic and inorganic part, which can be used to tune luminescence processes and their efficiency. Further tuning can be achieved by heterometallic assemblies or doping with different metal ions. The latter can be essentially utilized to increase efficiency by reduction of quenching effects. The porosity of MOFs offers the benefit to incorporate luminescence centres into the pore system. This can be used to achieve luminescence in intrinsically non-luminescent frameworks or vary an intrinsic luminescence.
The variability of luminescence effects in MOFs and CPs opens new possibilities for their use in lighting, sensing or sensors as currently emerging fields.
With this tutorial review we want to offer a basic insight into luminescent CPs and MOFs, their variability and possible construction as well as limits due to contradictory effects like quenching, focusing on metal based processes and being suitable for a broader readership.
